From a5c42c081044c02f143c42145124112353ea4375 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Baryshkov <dmitry.baryshkov@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 10:47:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/19] freedreno/a5xx: convert 2D blits through the right
 intermediate format

A converting 2D blit wider than a dword comes back wrong on a530: rgba8
to r8 is correct, rgba32f to r32f is not, and neither is r32f to rg32f.
The blit was restricted to formats of at most four bytes because of it.

{RB,GRAS}_2D_BLIT_CNTL is written as the constant 0x86000000, and bits
28:30 of that are the intermediate format the 2D core converts through,
the same field, with the same encoding, that fd6_ifmt() selects on a6xx.
The constant holds R2D_UNORM8, so every blit converted through 8-bit
unorm no matter what it carried, and anything whose channels were not
8-bit unorm shaped came out wrong.

Describe the register in the XML and derive it from the destination
format as a6xx does, then drop the size restriction it was hiding
behind. Measured on a db820c against u_blitter over every ordered pair
of 20 renderable formats, tiled and linear, with a repeat run as a noise
control: every pair above a dword (rgba32f to r32f, r32f to rg32f,
rgba32f to rg32f and the rest) now passes.

The remaining fp16 divergence is unrelated: it corrupts the destination
by a factor of exactly 2^-16 with the added channel filled with a fixed
pattern, which is a mis-strided copy rather than a conversion, and it is
unaffected by this field. Those pairs stay rejected.

